# Frontmatter

A document can start with YAML frontmatter between two `---` lines.

::::plate[The `metadata` export](mark="---", tone=text)
The frontmatter is exported from the document's module script as `metadata`, so the document can read it and so can anything that imports the document:

```svx result file="src/routes/article/+page.svx"
---
title: My lovely article
author: Dr. Fabuloso the Fabulous
tags: [svelte, markdown]
---

# {metadata.title}

By {metadata.author}.
```

```js
import { metadata } from './article.svx';
```

:::notes
[Templates](/docs/guides/templates#template-props) get every key as its own prop.
:::

:::edges
- Keys are not separate variables, so write `{metadata.title}` rather than `{title}`.
:::
::::

::::plate[Listing posts](mark="glob", tone=expr)
To list posts in SvelteKit, import their metadata with `import.meta.glob` and the `?metadata` query:

```js title="src/routes/blog/+page.js"
const posts = import.meta.glob('./*/+page.svx', {
	query: '?metadata',
	import: 'metadata',
	eager: true
});

export function load() {
	return {
		posts: Object.entries(posts).map(([path, metadata]) => ({
			slug: path.split('/')[1],
			...metadata
		}))
	};
}
```

:::notes
A `?metadata` import reads only the frontmatter, so the list does not load the component of every page.
:::
::::

::::plate[In a layout](mark="route", tone=expr)
A SvelteKit layout can read the frontmatter of the page it wraps the same way. Without `eager`, each page's metadata loads on its own, and the id of the route picks it:

```js title="src/routes/+layout.js"
const pages = import.meta.glob('./**/+page.svx', {
	query: '?metadata',
	import: 'metadata'
});

export async function load({ route }) {
	// the root route is `/`, every other id has no trailing slash
	const dir = route.id === '/' ? '' : route.id;
	const metadata = await pages[`.${dir}/+page.svx`]?.();

	return { metadata: metadata ?? null };
}
```

```svelte title="src/routes/+layout.svelte"
<script>
	let { data, children } = $props();
</script>

<svelte:head>
	<title>{data.metadata?.title ?? 'My site'}</title>
</svelte:head>

{@render children()}
```

:::notes
`data.metadata` is `null` on a route that isn't a document, and on a document with no frontmatter. The same `load` works in a `+layout.server.js`.
:::
::::

## TypeScript

::::plate[Types for these imports](mark=".d.ts", tone=expr)
`mdsvex/globals` in `compilerOptions.types`, beside `vite/client`, types the query imports: `?metadata`, `?data` and `?text`.

A plain `./post.svx` import is a file TypeScript can't read itself. In the editor, the mdsvex extension's [TypeScript plugin](/docs/reference/packages#editor-tools) reads it, with the exports the document has. `tsc` loads no plugins, so a type check on the command line needs a declaration in the project. A [`?template=`](/docs/guides/templates#opting-out) import is not a file at all, so it needs one in the editor too:

```ts title="src/svx.d.ts"
declare module '*.svx' {
	import type { Component } from 'svelte';
	const component: Component;
	export default component;
	export const metadata: Record<string, unknown> | undefined;
}

declare module '*?template=false' {
	import type { Component } from 'svelte';
	const component: Component;
	export default component;
	export const metadata: Record<string, unknown> | undefined;
}
```

:::notes
A module pattern holds one `*`, so each `?template=` value you import with needs a block of its own. The file has no top-level `import`, so its blocks declare modules rather than add to them.
:::
::::

## Supported YAML

::::plate[The built-in parser](mark="key:", tone=text)
mdsvex parses frontmatter itself, with no dependency, so `compile` still runs anywhere. It understands the YAML that frontmatter tends to use:

- plain, single quoted and double quoted strings, including escapes in double quotes;
- numbers (`1`, `-4.2`, `1e3`, `0x1F`, `0o17`, `.inf`, `.nan`), booleans and `null` or `~`, as in YAML 1.2, so `yes` and `off` stay strings;
- dates, which stay strings, such as `2026-10-02`;
- nested maps, block sequences (`- a`), flow sequences (`[a, b]`) and flow maps (`{ a: 1 }`);
- literal `|` and folded `>` blocks, with `-` and `+` chomping;
- comments.

```svx result
---
title: 'Hello: World'
count: 0x1F
draft: off
published: 2026-10-02
author: { name: Ada, handle: ~ }
tags:
  - svelte
  - markdown
summary: >-
  Two lines,
  folded into one.
---
```

:::edges
These are errors that name the line, and point you at [`frontmatter.parse`](/docs/guides/frontmatter#custom-frontmatter-parsers):

- anchors (`&`), aliases (`*`), tags (`!!str`), complex keys (`?`) and merge keys (`<<`);
- indenting with tabs;
- a duplicate key;
- frontmatter that isn't a map of keys to values;
- a plain value containing `: `, such as `title: Hello: World`. Quote it: `title: 'Hello: World'`.

```svx error
---
title: Hello: World
---
```
:::
::::

## Custom frontmatter parsers

::::plate[`frontmatter.parse`](mark="parse", tone=expr)
`frontmatter.parse` replaces the built-in parser. It gets the raw text between the `---` lines and returns an object of metadata. Returning `null` or `undefined` means no metadata.

For full YAML, use a YAML library such as [`yaml`][yaml]:

```js config
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { parse } from 'yaml';

mdsvex({
	frontmatter: { parse: (raw) => parse(raw) }
});
```

```svx result config
---
author: &ada Ada
editor: *ada
---
```
::::

::::plate[A slug from the path](mark="file", tone=expr)
`frontmatter.parse` gets a second argument, `{ filename }`, the path of the document. A value that comes from where the document lives, such as its slug, can go into `metadata` there:

```js config
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { parse } from 'yaml';

mdsvex({
	frontmatter: {
		parse: (raw, { filename }) => ({
			slug: filename?.split('/').at(-2),
			...parse(raw)
		})
	}
});
```

```svx result config file="src/routes/blog/hello-world/+page.svx"
---
title: Hello world
---
```

:::notes
The same parser runs for the compiled document, for a [`?metadata`](#listing-posts) import and for the `ctx.frontmatter` that [plugins](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#plugin-handlers) read, so all three see the slug. A `slug` written in the frontmatter wins here, because it is spread after.
:::

:::edges
- `filename` is `undefined` when whoever compiles the document gives no path, as `compile()` does without its `filename` option. The key is always there.
:::
::::

::::plate[TOML](mark="=", tone=text)
The fences stay `---`, but what goes between them is up to the parser. For TOML, with [`smol-toml`][smol-toml]:

```js config
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { parse } from 'smol-toml';

mdsvex({
	frontmatter: { parse: (raw) => parse(raw) }
});
```

```svx result config
---
title = "TOML Example"

[owner]
name = "some name"
---
```
::::
